Yeah. Those two fit together if we keep them grounded.

“Create an offer to pay for everything you buy.” That’s a strong economic kata. Instead of defaulting to:

\text{Need money}\rightarrow\text{sell hours}\rightarrow\text{buy thing}

ask:

\text{Want X}
\rightarrow
\text{What value can I create for whoever provides X?}
\rightarrow
\text{Offer}
\rightarrow
\text{Exchange}.

Money remains useful—it is the universal routing layer—but it doesn’t have to be the only intermediate representation. An offer can combine money, skill, access, introductions, promotion, labor, inventory, knowledge, risk-sharing, financing, or some new value you create. The constraint is that the other party freely values the exchange.

So I’d sharpen “don’t exchange life for money” into:

\boxed{\text{Don't sell time by default when you can build value that keeps working after the hour ends.}}

You can spend some life earning money; that’s normal. The trap is forgetting that time is the scarce underlying asset and treating money as the terminal objective rather than a coordination tool.

And “what you think about, you become” gets the same Reality Stitch. Thoughts aren’t magic commands to reality. But repeated attention alters what gets noticed, rehearsed, remembered, practiced, and eventually made habitual:

\text{Attention}
\rightarrow
\text{Repetition}
\rightarrow
\text{Representation}
\rightarrow
\text{Choice}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Habit}
\rightarrow
\text{Identity evidence}.

So perhaps the Pantheon version is:

\boxed{\text{What you repeatedly attend to becomes easier to see, easier to choose, and easier to become.}}

Which connects directly to your previous goal insight.

Don’t merely choose goals for what they’ll give you.

Choose what deserves sustained attention partly for what sustained attention will make of you.

And don’t merely ask:

How much does it cost?

Ask:

What can I create that makes this exchange worthwhile for both of us?

That’s the difference between thinking only like a buyer of the world and learning to become one of its producers.